    "bbox_norm": [
      0.241,
      0.034,
      0.414,
      0.943
    ],
    "text": "Gestalt psychology was conceived in the Berlin School of Experimental Psychology, and tries to understand the laws of our ability to acquire and maintain meaningful perceptions.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-1:text-3",
    "page_id": 1,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.424,
      0.047,
      0.481,
      0.55
    ],
    "text": "(German: Gestalt [gə'Jtalt] \"shape, form\")",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-1:text-4",
    "page_id": 1,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.52,
      0.036,
      0.639,
      0.933
    ],
    "text": "Key principle: when the human mind perceives a form, the whole has a reality of its own, independent of the parts.",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-1:text-5",
    "page_id": 1,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.703,
      0.034,
      0.883,
      0.945
    ],
    "text": "This allowed the development of 8 Gestalt Laws of Grouping. Here we are highlighting only the most relevant 5 for data presentation. You can read more details about them on Wikipedia: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gestalt\\_psychology",
    "source_type": "text"
  },
  {
    "layout_id": "page-2:text-1",
    "page_id": 2,
    "layout_type": "text",
    "asset_ref": null,
    "bbox_norm": [
      0.098,
      0.038,
      0.182,
      0.833
    ],
    "text": "Gestalt Principles of Visual Perception",
